    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Fracture permeability of rocks is a function of the geometry, aperture, connectivity, and spacing of fractures in natural fracture networks. Fluid flow through fractured rock is therefore strongly dependent on the type of structures that form in response to the applied stress field. Field observations in granites in the central Sierra Nevada in conjunction with model results are used to determine the characteristics of fluid flow and associated heat transport in a natural system.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
